      "id": 1,
      "text": "Scotts originated as Scotts Crossroads in 1873, a rural hub where farmers and travelers met at key junctions.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/scotts_north_carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened March 21, 1873, named Scotts Cross Roads, with Rufus W. Feimster as first postmaster; name shortened to Scotts in 1894.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24,
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/scotts_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Scotts sits 8 miles west-northwest of Statesville, 45 miles northwest of Charlotte, within the Charlotte‑Concord‑Gastonia metro area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/scotts_north_carolina",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Scotts lies in the Piedmont region, its undulating terrain and mixed hardwood forests support local agriculture and creek systems feeding Lake Norman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/scotts_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1866 Scotts School served African American children through eighth grade, fostering community growth until 1968.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/scotts_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Early settlers were mainly Scotch‑Irish and German, establishing homesteads in southern Iredell County after 1749.",
